## The question

{{ status_line(status, R, say, vy, r.year, HY) }}{% if recommendation %} The claims this result touches are withdrawn: {{ withdrawn(recommendation.withdrawn) }}. Recommendation to the owner, in the monitor's form: **{{ say.recommendation[recommendation.kind] }}**; the model in use next is his decision.{% endif %} Can a forecast of Québec's hourly electricity demand be trusted, and what would show that it should stop being trusted? This project does not look for the best forecast: it checks this one the way a model-risk team would. It measures its error on months it has not seen, checks what its ranges are worth, then replays whole years as in production, under a monitor that can recommend investigating the data, recalibrating the ranges or retraining the model. The monitor recommends; it never acts. ({{ sec(1) }})

## The forecast

At the end of each day, v2 forecasts the next day's 24 hours from a temperature forecast: that of NOAA's National Blend of Models for Montréal's airport, already issued at that time, no longer the day before's temperature as in v1. That one point stands for the whole province. v2 turns this forecast into demand through one temperature curve for each hour of the day, beside the hour, the day type and the demand of the day before and of the week before. Each month from {{ vy[0] }} to {{ vy[1] }} is forecast by a model fitted on the hours before it only. Over the {{ sel.hours|num }} hours where the three forecasts exist, the mean error is {{ sel.mape[prod]|pct(2) }} for v2, {{ sel.mape[fam]|pct(2) }} for v1's form on the same forecast and {{ sel.mape[ship]|pct(2) }} for v1 as shipped. These years served to choose v2: {{ choice_bias(sel) }}. With the temperature actually observed at the target hour, as if the weather were perfectly forecast, v2's mean error would be {{ P.gam_observed.overall.mean|pct(2) }}: an optimistic bound the forecast does not have. ({{ sec(2) }}, {{ sec(3) }})

