## How much confidence does it deserve?

Each forecast comes with a range that aims to hold demand {{ nominal|pct(0) }} of the time, its nominal level; nothing guarantees that it does, so its coverage is measured. From {{ v.conformal.first_fold }} to {{ v.folds.last }} ({{ label(v.contrasts.label) }}), v2's held it {{ C.relative.overall.mean|pct(1) }} of the time{% if sides.overall.under %}: less than the nominal, beyond the uncertainty of the measurement{% elif sides.overall.over %}: more than the nominal, beyond the uncertainty of the measurement{% else %}: the gap to the nominal stays within the uncertainty of the measurement{% endif %}. {% if sides.season.under or sides.decile.under or HBS.under %}It falls short where demand leaves it more often than the nominal allows, beyond that uncertainty: {{ groups(sides.season.under, sides.decile.under, HBS.under) }}.{% else %}No season, no hour block and no forecast-load decile leaves it more often than the nominal allows beyond that uncertainty.{% endif %} {% if sides.season.over or sides.decile.over or HBS.over %}It is wider than needed where demand stays inside more often than the nominal, beyond that uncertainty: {{ groups(sides.season.over, sides.decile.over, HBS.over) }}.{% else %}In no group does demand stay inside more often than the nominal beyond that uncertainty.{% endif %}{% if sides.season.under %} The coverage thresholds of {{ groups(sides.season.under, []) }} are drawn from weeks that already had this defect: the monitor does not flag it as a degradation.{% endif %} Coverage is never a criterion of the status. ({{ sec(5) }}{% if sides.season.under %}, {{ sec(10) }}{% endif %})

## {{ r.year }}, replayed but already seen

The year {{ r.year }} was replayed hour by hour, as in production. The model, frozen on the data up to {{ f24.params.train_last_day }}, was not refitted, and every read, temperature forecasts included, went through a clock that refuses any later data. {% if departures %}v2's frozen rules were changed after v2's first {{ r.year }} replay (below); this replay was run again after that change: it is **post hoc**. {% endif %}v2's mean error was {{ r.summary.mape.mean|pct(2) }}, and its {{ nominal|pct(0) }} range held demand {{ r.summary.coverage.mean|pct(1) }} of the time over the year. {% set WW = weekly_coverage.seasons.get('winter') %}{% if WW and WW.below %}Week by week, it held demand less often than the nominal in {{ WW.below }} of the {{ WW.judged }} winter weeks judged.{% elif WW %}Week by week, it held demand at least as often as the nominal in each of the {{ WW.judged }} winter weeks judged.{% endif %}{% if WW %} These weekly shares carry no interval and decide nothing.{% endif %} {% if not recs24 %}The monitor recommended nothing all year.{% elif recs24|length == 1 %}The monitor made one recommendation: {{ recs_list(recs24) }}.{% else %}The monitor made {{ recs24|length }} recommendations: {{ recs_list(recs24) }}.{% endif %} Against v1 as shipped, the verdict is "{{ VERDICT[r.verdict.shipped] }}"; against v1's form on the same forecast, "{{ VERDICT[r.verdict.family] }}". But {{ r.year }} is not a test: the developers had seen it through v1 before choosing v2. {% if r.verdict.shipped in ('not_shown_better', 'worse') or r.verdict.family in ('not_shown_better', 'worse') %}A verdict that is not "better" on a year already seen is a finding against v2.{% else %}A success on a year already seen is weak evidence, which supports no claim.{% endif %} ({{ sec(8) }})

