## Is silence good news?

A silent monitor reassures only if it can see a real change. The report measured that by imposing sustained changes at a few fixed starts and replaying the real rules. On {{ r.year }} ({{ LABEL[r.contrasts.label] }}), {% if LR.every_from is not none %}a sustained gap of ±{{ LR.every_from|pct(0) }} or more between demand and forecast yielded "retrain" within {{ K.power_horizon }} weeks at each of the {{ LR.starts }} starts{% else %}even the largest gap measured between demand and forecast, ±{{ LR.sizes[-1]|pct(0) }}, did not yield "retrain" within {{ K.power_horizon }} weeks at every one of the {{ LR.starts }} starts{% endif %}; a gap of ±{{ LR.smallest|pct(0) }} did at {{ LR.smallest_by_sign.up.caught + LR.smallest_by_sign.down.caught }} of {{ LR.smallest_by_sign.up.cases + LR.smallest_by_sign.down.cases }} cases. The forecast leans on the day before's demand: it follows part of a lasting rise in demand. The gap the rise opens between the two is therefore smaller than the rise. {% if level is not none and level24 is not none %}A sustained {{ level.size|pct(0) }} rise yielded "retrain" within {{ K.step_horizon }} weeks at {{ level.up_n - level.up_missed }} of {{ level.up_n }} starts on {{ p.year }} (the rehearsal year), and at {{ level24.up_n - level24.up_missed }} of {{ level24.up_n }} on {{ r.year }} (seen by the developers). {% set missed_any = LR.every_from is none or LR.every_from != LR.smallest or LP.every_from is none or LP.every_from != LP.smallest or level.up_missed or level24.up_missed %}{% if missed_any %}Silence is therefore no proof: sustained changes went unnoticed at some of the starts measured.{% else %}At each of the starts measured, on {{ p.year }} as on {{ r.year }}, every sustained gap and every {{ level.size|pct(0) }} rise imposed was caught; these are only a few starts.{% endif %}{% else %}The level-shift measures did not finish on each of the two years: silence is no proof.{% endif %} ({{ sec(7) }}, {{ sec(8) }})

## The honesty ledger

What changed since v1: the temperature input, a forecast instead of the day before, and the model, one curve per hour. Both were chosen on {{ span(vy) }}, after the developers had seen v1's errors on {{ r.year }}, its departure and a preliminary trial of six model families on those same years; at that time nobody had computed a v2 model on {{ r.year }} or read any demand of {{ HY }}. That is why {{ span(vy) }} and {{ r.year }} prove nothing: only {{ HY }} can. {% if not held %}**Pending:** {{ HY }} will judge, on held-out data, the verdicts against v1 as shipped and against v1's form on the same forecast, the criteria that could still show v2 wrong; they stay pending as long as those data do not exist.{% elif not R.finished %}The {{ HY }} test of record started and did not finish: nothing is claimed from it.{% else %}The {{ HY }} test of record finished, its standing "{{ OUTCOME[R.standing] }}" logged by its gate before it read any data; its verdicts: "{{ VERDICT[R.verdict.shipped] }}" against v1 as shipped, "{{ VERDICT[R.verdict.family] }}" against v1's form ({{ label(R.evaluation) }}).{% endif %} {% if departures %}v2's frozen rules were changed by a departure recorded in [`AGENTS.md`](../../AGENTS.md), and the full report shows the replay from before beside the one from after.{% else %}v2's rules were frozen (tag `{{ freeze.freeze_tag }}`, commit `{{ freeze.freeze_commit|short }}`) before v2's {{ r.year }} replay, and no departure has changed them since. What backs that order is the annotated tag, unsigned, whose date the developers' own tools recorded, and the start times the replay ledger records: nothing outside the repository anchors it.{% endif %} Every {{ r.year }} or {{ HY }} replay finished since the repository's history was rewritten is entered in the replay ledger, [`docs/replay-ledger.json`](../replay-ledger.json) (the {{ p.year }} rehearsal is not), and only v2's entries carry an evaluation label: an entry is never rewritten, and a replay that does not match its entry makes the report refuse. ({{ sec(8) }}, {{ sec(9) }})

What the report does not show: an independent human review of the code (everything comes from AI agents under the direction of a single owner), the soundness of NOAA's model itself, the drift false-alarm rate in {{ r.year }}, or the forecasts' real arrival delays, which are assumed. Other limits hold throughout. One forecast point, Montréal's airport, stands for the whole province. NOAA's model changes over the years are not tracked, so the forecast series is not homogeneous. No revision of any source is modelled. The day before's demand is taken as known when the forecast is issued, with the value the download holds and no publication delay. ({{ sec(10) }})
